# Allowlist for `resq secrets`. One pattern per line, `#` starts a comment.
# A finding is suppressed when its file path or its content contains a pattern.
# Read from the repo root by default (secrets.rs:786); override with --allowlist.
#
# Mirrors the "# Tools" entry resq-software/resQ has carried for the same file.

# The scanner's own source. build_rules() is a table of the very patterns it
# searches for — PEM private-key armour headers, AWS key-id prefixes, GitHub
# token prefixes and the rest — and the test module carries fixtures shaped like
# real credentials so the rules are exercised honestly. Every one is a regex
# literal or a synthetic test input; none is a credential.
#
# The patterns are described here rather than quoted: writing one out verbatim
# makes this allowlist match its own rules, which is how the first draft of this
# file ended up flagged on line 8.
#
# Without this entry `resq secrets --staged` fails on any commit that touches
# this file, so the pre-commit hook blocks all further work on the scanner
# itself. Seven of its own private-key rules match their own definitions.
crates/resq-cli/src/commands/secrets.rs
